00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:12.030
 I'm here today with Chris Bacon and Mario Eugenia Flores Gomez.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:21.070
 Chris Bacon is an environmental social scientist whose work has focused on food security and food sovereignty in northern Nicaragua, and more recently in California.

00:02:21.070 --> 00:02:27.350
 He takes a participatory action research approach to his work, and is a professor at Santa Clara University.

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:39.050
 Mario Eugenia Flores Gomez is a social psychologist and community organizer with more than 15 years of experience working in Central America and California for peace, human rights, and food security.

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:42.110
 So, welcome to Delicious Revolution, Chris.

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:44.110
 Thank you. Great to be here.

00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:51.110
 So, I just wanted to start talking about working on food security from two different perspectives.

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:54.430
 And can you talk a little bit about your work together?

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:58.430
 You're a husband and wife team, and you've collaborated a lot.

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:01.940
 But also, your work is a lot about collaboration.

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:10.280
 Can you just talk a little bit about how the collaboration between you two started and how that's grown?

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:15.580
 Well, that's a big question to start out with, Devin.

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:36.730
 But I would say that the work started really, to borrow the words of Mario Eugenia's dad, when my parents were meeting her parents and we were talking about getting married, thanks to the blessings from some larger spirit and because of the revolution.

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:51.750
 Because in Nicaragua, that's the revolution I'm talking about right now, specifically the revolution to overthrow in 1979, the Samosa dictatorship, which had run the country like a large plantation for about 45 years.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:04:15.630
 And that's what brought my mom to Nicaragua back in the 1980s, doing solidarity work to slow the sale of arms to an exacerbation of the violence in the region and to support the more transformative aspects of the revolution, like the literacy campaigns and women's rights.

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:29.320
 And so that's why I, when I received a call, after I applied to Peace Corps, I was living in Washington, D.C., after my undergraduate work, and I got an offer from Peace Corps to go to Nicaragua or to Panama.

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:31.320
 That's why I chose to go to Nicaragua.

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:43.330
 And after my experience in northern Nicaragua as a Peace Corps volunteer, I certainly, that has taught me Spanish, a lot about cultural differences and deeper reflections I need to on my own.

00:04:43.330 --> 00:04:52.290
 And then coming back to the United States, getting involved in organizing around fair trade, doing anti-oppression training as part of that work.

00:04:52.290 --> 00:05:09.640
 And going back to Nicaragua again before we met, in fact, I was working on a dissertation research looking at the impact of fair trade and organic coffee as a mitigation strategy in the context of the depression, the global prices of coffee.

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:21.980
 And so I was doing that work, working with the cooperatives, when I went out to dinner at an Italian restaurant with a bunch of the guys and the agronomists and the marketing consultants from there.

00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:28.140
 And there was only one big table in this Italian restaurant in Nicaragua.

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:29.140
 And there was no other tables.

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:31.140
 And half the tables were already taken up.

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:33.140
 And they're like, look, there's not enough room for any more seats.

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:36.330
 But if you want to share the other half of the table, you can go to this table.

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:42.450
 And so I was on the other side, one end of the table with sort of my crew from the co-ops, largely male.

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:49.470
 And the other end of the table, there was this beautiful woman and a couple other friends also that were working with Mario Henia.

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:51.470
 And I'll let her tell you what they were up to.

00:05:51.470 --> 00:06:00.620
 But the short story is there was a mutual friend who worked at a women's health clinic and had done a lot of alternative medicine in Nicaragua.

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:03.620
 And she was on Mario Henia's end of the table.

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:08.610
 So that was how we got the introduction for this intercultural collaboration.

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:18.710
 And from my perspective, I would say that collaboration and solidarity is what you learn when you grew up in a context of civil war.

00:06:18.710 --> 00:06:23.710
 I grew up in a really tough region in Nicaragua during the 80s.

00:06:23.710 --> 00:06:28.710
 And it's the northern border of Nicaragua that's really close to Honduras.

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:37.830
 And since I think all my memories are related with collaboration, you needed to collaborate in order to survive.

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:42.110
 And then, yes, you learn more approach and techniques.

00:06:42.110 --> 00:06:51.590
 And by the time that I met Chris, I was already learning how to collaborate with more like bigger approach,

00:06:51.590 --> 00:07:02.620
 the rural and urban collaboration, interdisciplinary collaboration, and all the bicultural, multicultural collaboration.

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:06.590
 I was working for the Canadian government at that time in Nicaragua.

00:07:06.590 --> 00:07:10.750
 And it was really interesting.

00:07:10.750 --> 00:07:18.040
 I will say, first, we learned collaboration and solidarity, and then we got the marriage.

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:24.040
 And then it took a couple of years for us to understand that we were working together.

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:29.100
 It was when one person, it is a roster, the Santa Cruz coffee roster.

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:34.010
 And she said, "Well, it seems like you are working really well together."

00:07:34.010 --> 00:07:38.010
 And we looked at each other and said, "Are we?"

00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:39.010
 And yes, we were.

00:07:39.010 --> 00:07:40.010
 And yes, we were.

00:07:40.010 --> 00:07:42.010
 We were working together.

00:07:42.010 --> 00:07:47.010
 And we were doing all kinds of research from counting trees.

00:07:47.010 --> 00:07:57.360
 We were conducting focus groups and working with United students for fair trade and different things with farmers.

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:03.420
 But I think we came to confront a different challenge when we become a family.

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:14.350
 And living in California and some months in Nicaragua and having a child who was 100% breastfeed child is a different situation.

00:08:14.350 --> 00:08:23.950
 And when she started talking, her vocabulary included words like seed banks, CADAs, that are the centers of food distribution in Nicaragua.

00:08:24.950 --> 00:08:29.950
 And droughts and crops and varieties and climate change.

00:08:29.950 --> 00:08:36.950
 And we were like, "Whoa, it seems like the whole family is working together, not just us as a couple."

00:08:36.950 --> 00:08:44.110
 But also, you have to face the rhythms and times and all kinds of things.

00:08:44.110 --> 00:08:54.050
 But it seems like still now that she's already six years old, we are proud that we are making it possible.

00:08:54.050 --> 00:08:55.850
 Yes.

00:08:55.850 --> 00:08:58.860
 How long have you been working on the food security project?

00:08:58.860 --> 00:08:59.860
 Six years.

00:08:59.860 --> 00:09:01.860
 Yeah, it's been six years.

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:07.300
 And I mean, I think that what makes a collaboration possible is really the willingness.

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:18.770
 You have to have the willingness to go and risk something and to, I mean, coming from California in the U.S. and to laugh at yourself and let people laugh at you.

00:09:18.770 --> 00:09:30.730
 And then also to be willing to have enough solidarity with the principles of the people that you're going to be working with to really to not try to objectively judge every single fact that is presented.

00:09:30.730 --> 00:09:33.730
 You need to triangulate the data that comes out.

00:09:33.730 --> 00:09:36.730
 But to also see like there is a real issue here, right?

00:09:36.730 --> 00:09:38.690
 And I think, and they'll test you.

00:09:38.690 --> 00:09:44.690
 Any good community that you're going to be collaborating with is going to test you multiple times to see the depth of your collaboration.

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:49.870
 And I think it's good to show them that you're willing to work in solidarity and give something up.

00:09:49.870 --> 00:09:54.870
 I mean, trust is about giving something up in anticipation of reciprocation over time, right?

00:09:54.870 --> 00:09:59.890
 And this is what Eleanor Ostrom, a Nobel economist, prize-winning economist, defined it as.

00:09:59.890 --> 00:10:06.890
 So you do to really trust you give something up with the idea that it's going to be some payback over time or some broader purpose that you're working for.

00:10:06.890 --> 00:10:09.820
 And before people let you in, they'll test you.

00:10:09.820 --> 00:10:10.850
 And I think it's healthy.

00:10:10.850 --> 00:10:27.960
 I mean, I still remember working before I met Mario Henia with a cooperative in Sumatagalpa, you know, and they, there was a time I wanted to do a focus at returning results back to farmers, sort of basic principle of good ethical research practice, as well as participatory action research.

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:35.920
 And I had this big event planned, and I was going to, like, give them the copies of their interviews, and we're going to talk about the processes of empowerment and uneven development.

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:37.920
 And there was issues in the co-ops, of course.

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:43.010
 And, you know, one of the leaders of the co-op, the president of the co-op at the time said, you know, we're not exactly sure what you're up to.

00:10:43.010 --> 00:10:44.010
 Can you cancel this thing, right?

00:10:44.010 --> 00:10:48.010
 And I was a little upset because it's all really hard to organize one of these things.

00:10:48.010 --> 00:10:50.970
 I did because that's what I was requested to do, and that's a strong partner.

00:10:50.970 --> 00:10:55.970
 And, of course, after I explained what I was doing, everybody was fine, and we did it exactly as I said.

00:10:55.970 --> 00:10:56.970
 And I always say what I see.

00:10:56.970 --> 00:11:01.940
 That's what I insist as a researcher is that you're able to say what you see to a broader audience.

00:11:01.940 --> 00:11:03.940
 And so there's always a negotiation.

00:11:03.940 --> 00:11:07.840
 In the Segovia's project, we got started.

00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:15.940
 So I had a relationship previously working with Proto Co-op, which is a leading smallholder cooperative organization in Nicaragua.

00:11:15.940 --> 00:11:21.940
 It represents about 2,300 families and about 10,000 people, over 1,100 certified organic producers.

00:11:21.940 --> 00:11:24.880
 35% are peanut farmers.

00:11:24.880 --> 00:11:29.260
 35% of the members currently, and they've continued to expand that.

00:11:29.260 --> 00:11:31.450
 And so I worked with them previously.

00:11:31.450 --> 00:11:39.730
 Previously, there was an opportunity because Green Mountain Coffee Roasters was starting to become more concerned about periods of seasonal hunger.

00:11:39.730 --> 00:11:46.730
 Because they finally read some of the papers that we've been writing, I've written, Ernesto Mendez, other folks, and traveled themselves.

00:11:46.730 --> 00:11:48.730
 Part of it was Rick Pizer, really.

00:11:48.730 --> 00:12:03.730
 And sort of realizing that the sort of dog and pony show that happens when donors or large scale coffee buyers come into town, like behind that happy smile, there's often a period of the year or moments like with all of us when things are really difficult.

00:12:03.730 --> 00:12:07.730
 One of those difficulties and the most common form of food insecurity is seasonal hunger.

00:12:07.730 --> 00:12:22.340
 So really, soon after Rosalia was born is when there was an agroecology short course in Vermont, and it's when I started drafting a project proposal for working with the Segovia's.

00:12:22.340 --> 00:12:25.340
 Madi Uheni had already introduced into the team.

00:12:25.340 --> 00:12:30.340
 I'm going to pass this to Madi Uheni because she has been leading the component of this project with CAN doing the development work.

00:12:30.340 --> 00:12:32.340
 And I've been leading the sort of research component of us.

00:12:32.340 --> 00:12:34.340
 Although it took us a while to sort of figure all these pieces out.

00:12:34.340 --> 00:12:39.340
 But the early stages is like Rosalia was a couple weeks, a couple months old.

00:12:39.340 --> 00:12:52.340
 And you were really, I was, we had a division of labor at that time that was gendered where I was working on writing some of this project, a lot of it, in collaboration with the folks at Product Co-op.

00:12:52.340 --> 00:13:03.370
 And essentially, the goal was, the reason that, one of the reasons we wrote is because I saw this persistence of seasonal hunger, a well-organized co-op that's transparent.

00:13:03.370 --> 00:13:08.400
 You know, if you had to name the top five fair trade co-ops in the world, you would pick Product Co-op every time.

00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:15.400
 I had a very strong charismatic leader who represents smallholder interests to anybody and everybody.

00:13:15.400 --> 00:13:17.460
 And so, Marilyn Paisa…

00:13:17.460 --> 00:13:22.460
 It was one of your findings as a researcher, but it's a well-known…

00:13:22.460 --> 00:13:23.460
 Yes.

00:13:23.460 --> 00:13:26.460
 Farmers, it's not a new topic for them.

00:13:26.460 --> 00:13:33.630
 When Chris presented his findings, they were, well, it's nothing new for us.

00:13:33.630 --> 00:13:38.600
 We have been living through this kind of seasonal hunger for generations.

00:13:38.600 --> 00:13:41.600
 We don't call it seasonal hunger.

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:46.010
 They call it Julio, that is July.

00:13:46.010 --> 00:13:48.970
 But the seasonal hunger period is extended.

00:13:48.970 --> 00:13:55.120
 The worst month is July, and this is the reason that they call it Julio.

00:13:55.120 --> 00:14:01.150
 And the indigenous perspective gives you all kinds of mechanisms to confront.

00:14:01.150 --> 00:14:16.410
 And we have rituals too, to confront, to prepare psychological, prepare yourself, prepare your whole family, prepare the community to confront and to live through the hard situation every single year.

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:18.490
 And it was nothing new.

00:14:18.490 --> 00:14:24.510
 It's just, okay, you are presenting something that really matched our experience.

00:14:24.510 --> 00:14:28.510
 And I think the new thing is about talking together.

00:14:28.510 --> 00:14:38.100
 Saying, okay, we have been talking about that, but it seems like nobody wanted to pay attention because it's so normal.

00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:41.100
 And it looks like it's not interesting for a project.

00:14:41.100 --> 00:14:52.100
 And this is the reason we have to concentrate on improving soil fertility or improve the coffee yields or improve, you know, how to be pest resistant.

00:14:52.100 --> 00:14:56.100
 Because this is the part that is important for the coffee buyers.

00:14:56.100 --> 00:14:59.320
 But hunger, they don't care about it.

00:14:59.320 --> 00:15:05.080
 And the time that we start talking, okay, let's open up this topic.

00:15:05.080 --> 00:15:09.080
 I think this was a new experience for everyone.

00:15:09.080 --> 00:15:11.070
 And saying, we are taking it seriously.

00:15:11.070 --> 00:15:19.100
 Because the other thing is when you do your research and come back, and I will say, no, everyone come back to present results.

00:15:19.100 --> 00:15:23.390
 Most of the time, people just do the research and never come back there.

00:15:23.390 --> 00:15:28.390
 Yeah, if you have access to read English, you will read a summary.

00:15:28.390 --> 00:15:36.580
 But most of the time, you even don't have access to internet to see what comes out about what you were supposed to interview for.

00:15:36.580 --> 00:15:43.900
 After the cell visit that we were talking about the same thing, they say, okay, it seemed like we were going deep in this situation.

00:15:43.900 --> 00:15:51.960
 And I think it took us a couple years, no, to really open up and start talking every time.

00:15:51.960 --> 00:16:05.960
 In the survey that we applied in the beginning of the project during the diagnostic, and we asked them about, do you have like recipes that you use during this period of seasonal hunger that you want to share with us?

00:16:05.960 --> 00:16:08.960
 We have almost 800 recipes we collected.

00:16:08.960 --> 00:16:10.210
 Yeah.

00:16:10.210 --> 00:16:12.130
 And it's not a lack of knowledge.

00:16:12.130 --> 00:16:14.520
 You know, there is a lot of knowledge.

00:16:14.520 --> 00:16:27.320
 And I think one piece that we try to say we put together is local knowledge, this academic knowledge, the NGO, local NGO, international NGO.

00:16:27.320 --> 00:16:34.380
 It's all this, when we start talking about collaboration, you know, it's okay how we put all those pieces together.

00:16:34.380 --> 00:16:45.560
 And I will talk right now about our protagonists, how it makes me sick when just one person wants to celebrate one thing and say, okay, it's about me.

00:16:45.560 --> 00:16:46.460
 No, it's not about me.

00:16:46.460 --> 00:16:48.840
 It's a collective effort.

00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:55.350
 And the thing that we have to celebrate is that, how we work together and start looking for solutions.

00:16:55.350 --> 00:17:00.670
 And I know that in California it's nothing new because people work together here too.

00:17:00.670 --> 00:17:01.670
 Yeah.

00:17:01.670 --> 00:17:14.630
 And all this relationship with Nicaragua, the solidarity that we were talking about before, how a lot of people from the United States were in solidarity with Nicaragua during the war and even before.

00:17:14.630 --> 00:17:19.530
 And I think you wanted to add something, no?

00:17:19.530 --> 00:17:29.950
 No, I would just say on the collaboration, I think building that collective vision that we did together really and taking that risk and realizing none of this would be possible.

00:17:29.950 --> 00:17:39.380
 I mean, I could, I couldn't have done any of this if there hadn't been collaboration within the family first and leadership and ideas, not just like taking care of the kids.

00:17:39.380 --> 00:17:49.380
 It's leadership and ideas rooted in both her academic training, as well as her cultural understanding of coming up in Nicaragua that Mario Henny has contributed, as well as coordinating the projects that's gone forward.

00:17:49.380 --> 00:18:00.520
 But it's also been the collaboration from the cooperative, from the local nonprofit, which is CSDNIC, and from the Community Iorecology Network, right?

00:18:00.520 --> 00:18:01.520
 Yes.

00:18:01.520 --> 00:18:09.520
 And I think in the beginning, the early challenge, because I think it's important to sort of see there's a couple key moments in the project, and maybe we should describe a little bit more of the broad contours.

00:18:09.520 --> 00:18:16.520
 But one key thing that I think goes to collaboration across the board is, is this going to be a collaboration?

00:18:16.520 --> 00:18:21.610
 To me, like getting things started, the question is, is this going to be another development project?

00:18:21.610 --> 00:18:27.740
 And that's the de facto starting point for many projects, because that's what people are used to.

00:18:27.740 --> 00:18:28.740
 They're doing too much.

00:18:28.740 --> 00:18:29.740
 There's time is too crunched.

00:18:29.740 --> 00:18:34.740
 And so you, you look at something and say, what can I get out of this as an, as an organization and as an individual?

00:18:34.740 --> 00:18:38.740
 And so we had to overcome and as, or as a researcher, right?

00:18:38.740 --> 00:18:42.740
 So it's not, you know, we're probably more guilty than most in the protagonism, right?

00:18:42.740 --> 00:18:49.740
 So we had to overcome that to say, can we collect, can we construct a common vision of what's possible?

00:18:49.740 --> 00:18:57.700
 And then can we be inspired not by that vision to do the hard work of personal transformation and organizational changes to make it happen?

00:18:57.700 --> 00:18:59.700
 And so we really bet on this partnership model.

00:18:59.700 --> 00:19:06.390
 We shared the budget for this whole project with the, with the community partner.

00:19:06.390 --> 00:19:09.390
 Madi Eugenia continues to do the same thing and negotiate.

00:19:09.390 --> 00:19:11.390
 They see the budget coming to Canada from the donor, right?

00:19:11.390 --> 00:19:14.740
 That's pretty rare in development work and many projects.

00:19:14.740 --> 00:19:24.830
 And we talk about the vision of, we, we set up a goal of ending seasonal hunger in this, in this region with these farmers in the Segovia is in a period of 10 years.

00:19:24.830 --> 00:19:26.830
 So we had a goal that we could all work for.

00:19:26.830 --> 00:19:33.300
 And, and I think that was part of establishing the collaboration of what would this, the, and I think I was, I totally agree with Madi Eugenia.

00:19:33.300 --> 00:19:39.320
 What is different people knew about seasonal hunger and we started opening the dialogue to talk about, talk about it.

00:19:39.320 --> 00:19:44.320
 And it's also what are, but even with all these projects and all these certifications, right?

00:19:44.320 --> 00:19:47.060
 Hunger persists.

00:19:47.060 --> 00:19:53.060
 And we were able to get this at a broader level on the agenda for the specialty coffee association of America.

00:19:53.060 --> 00:20:01.060
 And together with people like Rick Pizer and other folks launch a coalition to end hunger in the coffee, in the coffee lands everywhere, which still has a long way to go.

00:20:01.060 --> 00:20:05.060
 But we've made a little bit of progress, at least raising that dialogue within the industry.

00:20:05.060 --> 00:20:11.310
 But really the deep work is with the farmers in these communities and with the local other stakeholders in the process.

00:20:11.310 --> 00:20:16.340
 And I think laying out the strategy of the project, which might be of interest to the, to the listeners.

00:20:16.340 --> 00:20:20.400
 Um, I'll let you, um, I'll, I'll let you talk about the current strategy.

00:20:20.400 --> 00:20:26.750
 Well, I will go back to the, to the collaboration.

00:20:26.750 --> 00:20:34.640
 I think, uh, key point was to talk and also to sign.

00:20:34.640 --> 00:20:43.190
 We sign, we can, and product up and see as the need 10 years commitment to a start.

00:20:43.190 --> 00:20:55.630
 We say, okay, this is one project that we have a fund for three years, but we are willing to collaborate at least for 10 years to start seeing some positive results.

00:20:55.630 --> 00:21:01.650
 And, and, and we were open to that, even without having the money.

00:21:01.650 --> 00:21:12.660
 We were open, and, and, and, and I will recognize can as an organization who have been supporting, like openly supporting the, the work that we have been coordinating.

00:21:12.660 --> 00:21:25.810
 They are no, like, you know, sometime when you have worked for a US NGO, you have to become a, like a kind of supervisor and you have to go and make sure everything is working and this and that.

00:21:25.810 --> 00:21:29.810
 And you invest your time just writing reports for here is a completely different story.

00:21:29.810 --> 00:21:44.810
 They knew how we were working and they trust us and they support the work and always asking how everything's going, how we can, you know, put a little more energy toward the, how we can learn about the process that you have been coordinating there.

00:21:44.810 --> 00:21:45.810
 It was amazing.

00:21:45.810 --> 00:22:03.930
 We, we, we, we were feeling all the time, the support from the organization and not just asking us to, to, to, to show up the, the, the, the report and also the level of flexibility and how the farmers wanted to have some investment in one thing that was different than in the project.

00:22:03.930 --> 00:22:08.050
 And, and, and we were open to do that too.

00:22:08.050 --> 00:22:25.800
 It was a 15% chance to change inside the budget, some of the money and that flexibility is important because you can keep finding different challenges and you decide, okay, with the farmer, now this money has to be reallocated.

00:22:25.800 --> 00:22:38.140
 And we didn't expect the drought and say, okay, we need to buy more seeds because the variety has been disappearing to do farmers led experimentation.

00:22:38.140 --> 00:22:58.430
 Or we have to invest a little more money in food or we have to invest more money in how to improve the seed saving or buy more silos for the households because they need to, because all the poor harvest losses.

00:22:58.430 --> 00:23:11.720
 And, and they are so, and the strategy right now, I think they are more centered and related with the finding.

00:23:11.720 --> 00:23:16.720
 We found out that the fruit trees are important to confront seasonal hunger.

00:23:16.720 --> 00:23:29.720
 And we have been planting more than 20,000 fruit trees and we will, and some of them, I will say 15,000 of the fruit trees that were planted are already, they already have the first harvest.

00:23:29.720 --> 00:23:34.830
 We have lemons and oranges, I mean 18 varieties of the fruits.

00:23:34.830 --> 00:23:45.830
 And we are keep, we are in the vision that we want to keep planting more fruit trees because we know that it's important for the food and for sale and have extra incomes.

00:23:45.830 --> 00:23:52.830
 And the other is the soil fertility improvement, not just with one technique.

00:23:52.830 --> 00:23:59.740
 We have a combination from the agroecology perspective and we have a whole mosaic of possibilities.

00:23:59.740 --> 00:24:11.800
 And we have been working with relocalizing food during the period of hunger, also the period when the basic grains are really expensive.

00:24:11.800 --> 00:24:28.000
 And, and we realized with the farmer that it was important to buy the grains when they are cheap or cheaper and store them with agroecological methods.

00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:29.000
 So it means that we are not using chemicals.

00:24:29.000 --> 00:24:32.000
 It means that we are using the local knowledge.

00:24:32.000 --> 00:24:47.000
 So we are specifically using like the hojas de madero negro, like leaves from a madero negro tree and ash that are being mixed in with the grains or sometimes from neem trees and then taking them out and drying them periodically.

00:24:47.000 --> 00:24:54.000
 So different strategies for exact, for, for, for a grain storage to reduce the post harvest loss, crop loss.

00:24:54.000 --> 00:24:55.000
 And those are one of the strategies.

00:24:55.000 --> 00:24:56.000
 There's other ones too.

00:24:56.000 --> 00:24:57.020
 Yeah.

00:24:57.020 --> 00:25:00.020
 And they are, I will mention one simple.

00:25:00.020 --> 00:25:13.300
 When they want to plant seeds and the way that they cure the seeds is they mix garlic and they use like a natural dye to color the seeds.

00:25:13.300 --> 00:25:20.300
 Because in that way, when the birds see the seeds look like pinking, it is no attractive for them.

00:25:20.300 --> 00:25:23.300
 And they don't eat it because it looks different than corn.

00:25:23.300 --> 00:25:28.300
 And, and they have more level, more chance of survive on the soil.

00:25:28.300 --> 00:25:30.260
 And there is all kinds.

00:25:30.260 --> 00:25:31.260
 We can go.

00:25:31.260 --> 00:25:42.450
 The types of soil fertility kinds of strategies that we're talking about are, for example, using the microorganisms to dynamicize the, the, the, the spray on organic fertilizer.

00:25:42.450 --> 00:26:02.740
 And so sometimes that will go actually to the, to the surrounding forest, recover some of the leaves and the, and the top soil there, and then bring those, that soil back and then incubate in, in, in like a big 50 gallon barrel with the mixture of milk and molasses and other products.

00:26:02.740 --> 00:26:03.740
 They'll incubate the microorganisms that are in the soil.

00:26:03.740 --> 00:26:05.740
 They call it microorganisms that are in the soil.

00:26:05.740 --> 00:26:08.740
 They call it microorganismos de la montaña.

00:26:08.740 --> 00:26:22.760
 And then they will, after incubating those and, and basically growing a concentrated colony of these microorganisms, they will dilute it and use backpack sprayers to spray that on to increase the sort of the foliant growth and the, and fertilize the plants.

00:26:22.760 --> 00:26:27.760
 So these are some of the different strategies, these recipes that have been sort of moving around Central America over a period of time.

00:26:27.760 --> 00:26:35.820
 There's not a lot of empirical evidence, systematic research on the impact, but people see the evidence on, on their farms.

00:26:35.820 --> 00:26:38.820
 And these are some of the types of practices.

00:26:38.820 --> 00:26:56.520
 I think when we, when we talk about hunger and poverty, it's easy to imagine a kind of desperation where there's not a lot of possibility, but it sounds like what you're describing is like this explosion of creativity and experimentation as a way to use what's already there to, to address hunger.

00:26:56.520 --> 00:27:18.900
 So, so I wanted to, to hear a little about from you, like kind of starting at the beginning of the project, what did you find during those first surveys of like, what was the experience of hunger like, and then what has the response been, what kinds of strategies have come up, where did they come from and, and how is that, like, how's that open new possibilities over that time?

00:27:18.900 --> 00:27:26.240
 So I'll start by talking about some of the research from the first stage that was conducted in 2009, 2010.

00:27:26.240 --> 00:27:36.250
 And then perhaps Marihani can talk about the response in the project, or also even your own experience from coming from the region of what this period is like.

00:27:36.250 --> 00:27:44.240
 We found in that survey that there was an average of about three months, three months, three months and one week.

00:27:44.240 --> 00:27:55.240
 So we asked people, you know, is it is a whole long survey, which took, you know, went house to house to interviewing people in their homes, and asked them a whole set of agro, agroecological agronomic and livelihood based questions.

00:27:55.240 --> 00:28:05.240
 But in the, that one of the key results that was certainly served as an indicator for the work in the project was that people lived through about three months and three months and one week on average of hunger during the year.

00:28:05.240 --> 00:28:07.240
 Now, what is seasonal hunger actually mean?

00:28:07.240 --> 00:28:09.240
 And we've been learning about it as we go.

00:28:09.240 --> 00:28:12.240
 And it was interesting, the agronomists and the co-op weren't that aware of it.

00:28:12.240 --> 00:28:17.100
 And they knew kind of broadly about the Julio, right, but they didn't focus on it.

00:28:17.100 --> 00:28:23.100
 And so this was, are there, are there certain months where it's difficult for you to meet your basic food needs?

00:28:23.100 --> 00:28:33.190
 We didn't at that time begin to ask about the coping mechanisms, which helps us to, so those are those kind of like a time dimension understanding of seasonal hunger, right?

00:28:33.190 --> 00:28:41.150
 And the hunger, food security, as we know, has temporal dimension, has questions of access, food utilization, and food availability.

00:28:41.150 --> 00:28:44.150
 So in this temporal dimension, we realize there's an issue.

00:28:44.150 --> 00:28:58.320
 In subsequent interviews and focus groups, people said, we've used some of the same experience-based measures for food insecurity that are used by the World Food Program and designed, developed by people like Devereaux and others at the UN.

00:28:58.320 --> 00:29:10.920
 And that essentially talks about coping mechanisms in terms of skipping meals, eating less preferred foods, watering down the soups, selling crop harvest for a low price.

00:29:10.920 --> 00:29:15.920
 So a lot of times people are so strapped for cash during June, July, and August, if you look at the seasonal calendar.

00:29:15.920 --> 00:29:28.360
 After planting in May, it's before that first harvest comes in, after the last harvest, long after the harvest from the last corn and bean harvest has run out, and there's fewer opportunities for off-farm employment.

00:29:28.360 --> 00:29:45.360
 And so people would sell their future corn or even their future coffee harvest at very low prices to local intermediaries or giving them necessary cash, which may have been used to purchase food or for a medical expense or could have just been wasted, right?

00:29:45.360 --> 00:29:53.350
 But when we start to compare this, and this is where we pulled in some of the theory in a subsequent paper, is this is really accepting a low exchange entitlement.

00:29:53.350 --> 00:29:55.300
 So there is food available, right?

00:29:55.300 --> 00:30:05.600
 But there's not food being accessed in the types of food that they need or want or prefer in their homes during these critical months.

00:30:05.600 --> 00:30:18.980
 And so understanding their ability, the way that they're situated relative to the market and information flows and local organizations actually getting and linking with the farmers.

00:30:18.980 --> 00:30:26.320
 So the farmers we saw, sometimes farmers were selling, they would sell, for example, their future corn or their future coffee harvest to get a little bit of cash.

00:30:26.320 --> 00:30:34.350
 They'll say, okay, I will promise to sell you one quintal, 100 pounds of coffee, if you give me 200 pounds of corn right now.

00:30:34.350 --> 00:30:41.310
 And if you look at the prices in the market at the time when they're making these commitments, so they're expecting a two to one exchange.

00:30:41.310 --> 00:30:45.690
 But if you look at the prices in the market, they should have been getting eight or ten to one, right?

00:30:45.690 --> 00:30:53.360
 They should have been getting 800 pounds of corn now for offering a future 100 pounds of dry green coffee that's ready for export.

00:30:53.360 --> 00:31:01.380
 But they accepted these really awful terms of trade from their perspective because they needed the cash so bad.

00:31:01.380 --> 00:31:03.380
 And the farmers knew they were getting screwed.

00:31:03.380 --> 00:31:04.380
 We're getting screwed.

00:31:04.380 --> 00:31:08.790
 I remember an interview saying, we know we're getting screwed, but we have no alternative to access this.

00:31:08.790 --> 00:31:16.110
 Most of the time they can't get access to credit because credit would cost, the commercial banks won't loan to them.

00:31:16.110 --> 00:31:23.480
 And even intermediaries are going to be charging them up to 8% a month, a month, if they get any kind of credit, right?

00:31:23.480 --> 00:31:24.480
 So this is the kind of bind.

00:31:24.480 --> 00:31:27.480
 And so that's when people sometimes take more drastic coffee mechanisms.

00:31:27.480 --> 00:31:34.700
 What it looks like selling that future crop harvest could also look like selling part of your land, pulling your kid out of school and sending them somewhere else to work.

00:31:34.700 --> 00:31:35.700
 So you can get some extra revenue.

00:31:35.700 --> 00:31:39.700
 So I want to understand a little bit more about the depth of the lean months.

00:31:39.700 --> 00:31:54.660
 And I think this is why I've learned a lot from Mario Henny because she has actually growing up in that region and knowing people from both her own indigenous background and also from research that we have done can help us give a broader context for what the lean months actually mean in this context.

00:31:54.660 --> 00:32:03.960
 And then I'll tell you a little bit more about some of the research findings in terms of what we thought could be developed strategically coming out of this and then where it actually went can go back and forth.

00:32:03.960 --> 00:32:13.540
 I mentioned before that there is a lot of knowledge related with the seasonal hunger.

00:32:13.540 --> 00:32:20.890
 And we are talking about farmers that are small scale farmers.

00:32:20.890 --> 00:32:23.890
 It's a really small farm.

00:32:23.890 --> 00:32:27.470
 The average is two manzanas.

00:32:27.470 --> 00:32:29.850
 That means like one.

00:32:29.850 --> 00:32:30.850
 About three acres.

00:32:30.850 --> 00:32:32.430
 Even less.

00:32:32.430 --> 00:32:33.430
 Two and a half acres.

00:32:33.430 --> 00:32:34.390
 Less than that.

00:32:34.390 --> 00:32:44.610
 And it's hard to survive as a family who live from coffee from small farm.

00:32:44.610 --> 00:32:57.610
 And farmers in California know that even if they own a bigger farm because small farm here means like a lot bigger than the average small scale coffee farm in Nicaragua.

00:32:57.610 --> 00:33:10.910
 And there are a lot of families who don't own even a little piece of land to cultivate some crops and be able to have some food during the seasonal hunger.

00:33:10.910 --> 00:33:16.380
 And I grew up in that kind of family that didn't have land.

00:33:16.380 --> 00:33:27.550
 My dad used to work with four other farmers, bigger farmers to have a little bit of cash and sometimes just a little bit of corn.

00:33:27.550 --> 00:33:36.660
 And he never was able to provide enough food for the house.

00:33:36.660 --> 00:33:38.620
 But guess what?

00:33:38.620 --> 00:33:56.630
 The other thing that I found out is that the real farmer was my mother, who in a small piece of land, really tiny, the size of the, I will say, a little bit bigger than a backyard here.

00:33:56.630 --> 00:34:10.480
 She was able to cultivate at least 10 varieties of mangos, like 10 varieties of fruit trees, like citrus.

00:34:10.480 --> 00:34:21.550
 And she had like hundreds of vegetables, different types of vegetables, and corn, and to work, everything.

00:34:21.550 --> 00:34:22.550
 Yucca.

00:34:22.550 --> 00:34:28.700
 Yeah, everything, sweet potatoes, potatoes, yucca, cassava.

00:34:28.700 --> 00:34:37.700
 And we have squashes, different types of squashes, mustard, all kinds of leaf greens to eat.

00:34:37.700 --> 00:34:40.700
 And she had chickens, hundreds of chickens there.

00:34:40.700 --> 00:35:03.360
 And we had all, it was, I never had the sensation that I wasn't poor from the food kind of point of view, but it was because my mother, she was able to produce, but she was working constantly with the soil.

00:35:03.360 --> 00:35:08.810
 All the time feeding the soil, because it was her indigenous root.

00:35:08.810 --> 00:35:11.810
 And she was feeding the soil, and the soil was feeding us.

00:35:11.810 --> 00:35:13.810
 And preserving seeds.

00:35:13.810 --> 00:35:23.780
 I remember her, like saving the, helping us, we were helping her, and how to extract the seed, how to dry the seed, and save them for the next season.

00:35:23.780 --> 00:35:27.780
 Or roasting the pumpkin seeds, and eating them.

00:35:27.780 --> 00:35:30.130
 Things that people forget about.

00:35:30.130 --> 00:35:35.740
 You know, people worry about meat, and make sure the protein is there.

00:35:35.740 --> 00:35:45.890
 And they forget that we grew up having protein that is in the seeds, and spanking seeds, and beans, and other kinds of seeds.

00:35:45.890 --> 00:36:00.780
 And it was until I was in university that I realized, wow, you know, it was only my, you know, suppose that the father is the provider.

00:36:00.780 --> 00:36:08.680
 And he doesn't like to hear that he didn't provide enough, because I had a long conversation with him about that.

00:36:08.680 --> 00:36:15.670
 But, and how we twisted it around, it was, okay, we won't complain about that.

00:36:15.670 --> 00:36:24.290
 We will celebrate the fact that my mother is a farmer that was able to feed, we are 12.

00:36:24.290 --> 00:36:35.120
 She was able to feed all of us, to take care of us, because also my father was 10 years in the war.

00:36:35.120 --> 00:36:38.090
 And we needed to cultivate everything we had.

00:36:38.090 --> 00:36:44.890
 And, and I remember the other families saying, can you give me some mangoes?

00:36:44.890 --> 00:36:47.750
 Can you give me some squash?

00:36:47.750 --> 00:36:51.750
 And they have this same piece of land, just a tiny piece.

00:36:51.750 --> 00:36:55.780
 And my mother say, why they don't plant mango tree?

00:36:55.780 --> 00:36:58.780
 Why they don't plant an orange tree?

00:36:58.780 --> 00:37:04.600
 Because during the diagnostic, we start, we develop the whole spreadsheet.

00:37:04.600 --> 00:37:08.820
 Say, okay, if you plant this tree, what is the time that you harvest them?

00:37:08.820 --> 00:37:17.330
 And a lot of the fruit trees produce fruits during the period of hunger, July and August.

00:37:17.330 --> 00:37:22.330
 You have mangoes, you have oranges, you have jocote.

00:37:22.330 --> 00:37:24.330
 Since April, you have jocote.

00:37:24.330 --> 00:37:26.870
 And there is food.

00:37:26.870 --> 00:37:30.870
 But sometimes when we were talking with the farmers, they say, you know what?

00:37:30.870 --> 00:37:32.350
 We know that.

00:37:32.350 --> 00:37:38.350
 But it seems like something in between generations, we have been losing some of the knowledge.

00:37:38.350 --> 00:37:58.860
 And sometimes we just forget about, concentrate in the coffee, coffee production that we forgot about, that we need to make sure that the plant that produce food during the period of hunger, we need to make sure those plants have to be on the soil too, in order to have enough food.

00:37:58.860 --> 00:38:17.860
 I remember the question that helped get us started on that is when we were like, why are people paying to buy a Coca-Cola or a Pepsi in a local store way up in the top of the mountains, when they have almost no cash at all, while the mangoes are rotting on the ground?

00:38:17.860 --> 00:38:21.440
 And that's still a question that we're working to understand.

00:38:21.440 --> 00:38:26.840
 But it certainly has to do with this part of it, the answer.

00:38:26.840 --> 00:38:30.870
 And I think I'd like to do more ethnographic work too, thinking about how people manage their time.

00:38:30.870 --> 00:38:38.870
 But it certainly has to do a little bit with what Mario Henny was describing in terms of forgetting some of the useful strategies that they've used before.

00:38:38.870 --> 00:38:41.790
 And I would say in terms of...

00:38:41.790 --> 00:38:44.790
 One thing before you continue, the female participation.

00:38:44.790 --> 00:38:45.690
 Yeah.

00:38:45.690 --> 00:39:00.420
 I think food security, if you want to address food security, and this is not a new thing everyone has been talking about, that you have to include the female knowledge.

00:39:00.420 --> 00:39:03.460
 Not just because they are nursing the kids.

00:39:03.460 --> 00:39:05.780
 Which is where food security starts.

00:39:05.780 --> 00:39:09.060
 Yeah, it is food security.

00:39:09.060 --> 00:39:14.460
 But because they have a lot to say and a lot to teach others.

00:39:14.460 --> 00:39:19.840
 And it does open a little bit more the family conversation.

00:39:19.840 --> 00:39:27.790
 When we were asking, conducting some interviews about the most significant change, a lot of families say, you know what?

00:39:27.790 --> 00:39:33.840
 The most significant change in my family, now we are working as a family.

00:39:33.840 --> 00:39:36.760
 The mother is working in the garden.

00:39:36.760 --> 00:39:38.760
 The father is also working in the garden.

00:39:38.760 --> 00:39:42.920
 And the kids also have an important role to play.

00:39:42.920 --> 00:39:49.200
 And this is not just expecting that the male will provide the food.

00:39:49.200 --> 00:39:53.200
 And all my knowledge is not visible.

00:39:53.200 --> 00:39:57.200
 Nobody recognizes it and I have to be angry because of that.

00:39:57.200 --> 00:40:07.320
 And no, it's like we have to start talking and probably have years of experience talking about gender equality and all those kinds of things, but not related with food security.

00:40:07.320 --> 00:40:09.320
 And this is a new step.

00:40:09.320 --> 00:40:13.350
 We are including this topic inside the dialogue with them.

00:40:13.350 --> 00:40:23.720
 And it has been becoming very interesting in how they identify it as a positive change in their life.

00:40:23.720 --> 00:40:24.720
 And you can see it.

00:40:24.720 --> 00:40:26.720
 It's not just words.

00:40:26.720 --> 00:40:37.160
 You can see in facts that what families are working together and what families are in the process to work together in order to produce enough food to eat.

00:40:37.160 --> 00:40:59.350
 And I think related to that is when we were coming up after doing this diagnostic and this response to add some initial sort of strategic lines and are like trying to lay it out in a little bit as a as a strategy in the response, building off of local knowledge, linking agroecology and new strategies.

00:40:59.350 --> 00:41:14.690
 So it's not just only off of recovering what's in the past, because for whatever issue, the combination of the past and the current, you know, political economic paradigm and day to day interactions were not sufficient to eliminate these periods of seasonal hunger.

00:41:14.690 --> 00:41:15.690
 Right.

00:41:15.690 --> 00:41:15.790
 Right.

00:41:15.690 --> 00:41:21.000
 And so it's like, how do we combine the past with some interesting and potentially promising new strategies?

00:41:21.000 --> 00:41:22.060
 Right.

00:41:22.060 --> 00:41:30.060
 And so there was kind of agricultural diversification of the both the patio garden and and the potentially the Milpa as well.

00:41:30.060 --> 00:41:37.060
 And we'll talk about that as well as even some of the coffee farm.

00:41:37.060 --> 00:41:52.340
 There was also the idea of relocalizing the food system using the fair trade cooperatives as the organizational infrastructure that has historically been focused on export and purchasing coffee for export and credit.

00:41:52.340 --> 00:41:55.340
 Or they're marketing cooperatives, but they're actually multiple service cooperatives.

00:41:55.340 --> 00:42:01.330
 So they're legally and organizationally capable of doing a whole wide range of services.

00:42:01.330 --> 00:42:02.330
 They provide the technical assistance.

00:42:02.330 --> 00:42:08.330
 So we certainly work very closely with the agronomist and technical assistance team, including the gender promoter.

00:42:08.330 --> 00:42:26.260
 But the key component that we're interested in is how do we use a cooperative structure to relocalize the food system focusing on corn and beans and fruits and vegetables that could be how you give access to fresh, healthy food for all, particularly during those lean months.

00:42:26.260 --> 00:42:45.260
 So basically taking the fair trade concept of exporting the coffee and applying it to their own local food system focusing on corn and beans, where the co-op started to play a role in purchasing corn and beans from their own members, storing it in regional centers, and then redistributing it to these community based cooperative led grain and seed banks to their members.

00:42:45.260 --> 00:42:54.240
 And over the period of five or six years, over 100,000 pounds and more than that, 300,000 pounds has been redistributed.

00:42:54.240 --> 00:43:03.320
 The other part of the strategy, and we'll come back to this, is that Marihane was describing so well, and I think leading up to this, is both a gender and generational approach.

00:43:03.320 --> 00:43:11.670
 And so the gender-based approach is something we're continuing to learn and integrate closer and closer as a strategic response in the project.

00:43:11.670 --> 00:43:20.990
 And in the research, I will say my previous research has shown that there's uneven gendered response to the impacts of the coffee crisis and impacts of drought.

00:43:23.070 --> 00:43:33.970
 And that the gendered response to the current context of the coffee leaf rust, I haven't explored it empirically, right, through a systematic research.

00:43:34.170 --> 00:43:40.130
 We definitely saw certain correlates that are linked to seasonal hunger, and that was like the second phase of research.

00:43:40.330 --> 00:43:44.450
 And it was really cool to see how that was taken up in the project and run further.

00:43:45.310 --> 00:43:55.020
 So, for example, we ran a series of regression and statistical analysis, and we saw that, okay, what factors – there are some families that have shorter periods of seasonal hunger than others.

00:43:55.420 --> 00:44:00.490
 What factors are correlated with these families that are reporting shorter periods of seasonal hunger?

00:44:00.930 --> 00:44:07.270
 And the findings were pretty straightforward, some things that we expect that I think is good to remember as people involved in food movement work, right?

00:44:07.730 --> 00:44:08.470
 So, not surprising.

00:44:08.630 --> 00:44:12.670
 Some farmers that reported higher incomes reported shorter periods of seasonal hunger.

00:44:12.910 --> 00:44:14.490
 Some farms that were slightly larger.

00:44:15.210 --> 00:44:18.410
 Some farmers that reported doing more off-farm employment.

00:44:18.410 --> 00:44:31.400
 So, I think it's important to remember that some of these strategies, like off-farm employment and, you know, larger farms or higher incomes, whatever that could combine to mean, are important factors in reducing seasonal hunger, right?

00:44:31.400 --> 00:44:51.550
 So, we're not – and on the other hand, we also found that some of the households that reported shorter periods of seasonal hunger – and these are all statistically significant findings – were the ones that weren't necessarily selling more coffee, but were the ones that had produced more than half the food they eat on the farm, particularly corn and beans.

00:44:52.510 --> 00:44:57.210
 And to some extent – and this is what the fruit tree thing that Mario Henning keeps – we go back to.

00:44:57.270 --> 00:45:00.090
 This was found in a research done in 2009 and again in 2014.

00:45:01.050 --> 00:45:11.860
 Farmers that reported shorter periods of seasonal hunger had – also had more fruit trees reported present on their farm, an average of 35 to 40 as the number.

00:45:12.040 --> 00:45:20.950
 And so, this is where it was really cool to see a finding like this, discussing the finding, understanding it, doing the background research, pulling all the depth of knowledge already there.

00:45:20.950 --> 00:45:28.600
 And before – way before it got published, Mario Henning was already coordinating a campaign with the co-ops to plant, you know, over 20,000 fruit trees, right?

00:45:28.900 --> 00:45:32.260
 Now, that's pretty small compared to what could and needs to be done in the future.

00:45:33.090 --> 00:45:46.190
 So, I was really – and I think what's really cool about this, to come back around to this role of both gender and generational impact, is that the youth in these communities played a really important role, both in conducting the research and the interviews or the surveys.

00:45:46.190 --> 00:45:56.610
 I mean, who's going to go up and down these mountain hills and know where to go and also have the training to do a detailed answer where you're cross-checking the facts that come in and fill out like a 16-page survey, right?

00:45:56.730 --> 00:46:02.630
 You need a community member of child of these coffee farmers, many of them now that are in the university, even if it's part-time.

00:46:03.330 --> 00:46:20.670
 And so, they brought a wealth of knowledge, and they started through this process – and I'd like – maybe Maddie can talk more or I can talk about – how they also started to reflect on their own university training they were receiving and the knowledge that's held by their moms and their dads and their grandparents.

00:46:20.670 --> 00:46:24.770
 So, I think that generational question was really cool to start talking about.

00:46:24.770 --> 00:46:40.240
 One of the things I'm thinking about as I hear you talk about all these responses to dealing with hunger is that part of the things that you're talking about are, you know, how to make food available at a certain – at a lower price or make more food available.

00:46:40.240 --> 00:46:55.460
 But the other part of it is how to confront unfair power relationships, especially in gender and between campesinos and researchers, between men and women, between funders and practitioners.

00:46:55.720 --> 00:47:09.600
 I just think it's interesting you started this conversation saying that this is a collaboration born in revolution and that the revolutionary aspects of confronting power relationships is present in so many of these things that you do.

00:47:09.600 --> 00:47:13.290
 Well, I will say it's been not always easy.

00:47:16.290 --> 00:47:36.550
 And I've certainly had to, from my own work, you know, in sort of parsing things out a little, I've had to be very clear about what is the research component that's, you know, gone through the human subjects approval for the university and clearly explained to the farmers and the community that this information is going to be used for research.

00:47:36.550 --> 00:47:43.410
 Do I have permission to share some of it before it's published, you know, with our local partners, with CAN and Mario Henia and all the rest?

00:47:43.630 --> 00:47:47.470
 And that has been – and they get it.

00:47:47.530 --> 00:47:49.910
 Actually, it's really no problem for them at all.

00:47:50.170 --> 00:47:54.950
 The hard part in terms of the power structure has been interpreting that within the university, right?

00:47:54.950 --> 00:48:10.770
 So early on when I spent time collaborating, writing like this follow-up proposal for this project that was led as a whole team, right, with Protocol Up and Mario Henia and everybody there, you know, I said, well, look, we got a huge – you know, a large grant to do the second phase of the project for four years, five years, right?

00:48:12.090 --> 00:48:16.370
 But that didn't really count through the university because it wasn't run through the university, right?

00:48:16.430 --> 00:48:21.670
 And so I've also learned to sort of parse it out and explain up, right?

00:48:21.710 --> 00:48:28.560
 So explaining down, I think – or, you know, down in the hierarchy of society, not the way that I think about it.

00:48:29.140 --> 00:48:33.440
 But explaining to the farmers, the local co-ops, the NGOs, they pretty much get it.

00:48:33.460 --> 00:48:40.800
 They want me to sort of be clear about, you know, what's what, but they get it, that some is for research, but the research is going to be used for development and change.

00:48:40.800 --> 00:48:56.710
 But explaining this process up through the evaluation process has been a challenge, and I've finally figured out how to do it more and have – we'll be starting future work that's kind of more pure on the science side with still many implications for development and change.

00:48:56.950 --> 00:49:03.690
 Within our own relationship, I mean, navigating the power dynamics is not always easy either, and within the co-op as well.

00:49:03.790 --> 00:49:07.910
 I mean, there's all these different levels of these conflicts.

00:49:08.070 --> 00:49:20.070
 I think the biggest way, though, is to really get – for me, and I want to hear what you have to say about this too, Madi, is I think that people need to acknowledge these dynamics that are out there, first of all, right?

00:49:20.070 --> 00:49:33.440
 So historically, you know, NGOs have been sort of above the farmers, and they kind of get participation based on payment or not, and they justify and exaggerate their impacts.

00:49:33.680 --> 00:49:35.080
 And they've done a lot of great work too.

00:49:36.570 --> 00:49:39.760
 And the donors usually kind of run the show a little bit, right?

00:49:39.840 --> 00:49:44.200
 And I think in this case, what was interesting is it started out where we really pitched a partnership-based model.

00:49:44.840 --> 00:49:51.450
 And once – I think some of the stuff we're talking about earlier, we said we're willing to negotiate budgets and we're willing to really share vision.

00:49:51.510 --> 00:49:53.550
 But even then, we still had conflicts of knowledge, right?

00:49:53.810 --> 00:50:03.450
 When we started talking about home gardens, all the agronomists at the co-ops were thinking about like two rows of radishes in their back – in the backyard what they learned in agronomy school.

00:50:04.140 --> 00:50:08.590
 And, you know, from a research perspective, I was interested in, you know, what's the concept of home gardens here?

00:50:08.690 --> 00:50:15.490
 And what we realized is there needed to be a shared dialogue about even a basic interpretation of something like that.

00:50:15.490 --> 00:50:25.660
 And the way we addressed that really was through these agroecology short courses that are organized through the Community Agroecology Network and with Steve Gleesman and others that have helped put those on.

00:50:25.800 --> 00:50:27.600
 I think that – don't you think that's been pretty key for them?

00:50:27.620 --> 00:50:39.220
 Well, the training perspective, especially for the agronomists that we are calling them now agroecologists and was the international agroecology short course.

00:50:39.600 --> 00:50:50.410
 But for farmers, all our partnership with Programma de Campesino Campesino Pickup, it has been a key element.

00:50:50.890 --> 00:50:58.320
 And it took some time to establish this relationship because in the beginning was, is this a competition?

00:50:58.580 --> 00:51:03.640
 Is this kind of conversion you want to convert us to be a movement?

00:51:04.040 --> 00:51:05.800
 Or what is all this about?

00:51:06.040 --> 00:51:09.840
 Or you want us to apply the methodology that they're applying?

00:51:10.140 --> 00:51:28.640
 When the NDPCAC team was saying, we are not just a methodology, we are – and all this conversation, but we saw the potential and how to bring them together because they have a long history, but they are not working together, even if they are in the same region.

00:51:29.440 --> 00:51:36.410
 And it's easier when you are an academic to see a community organizer, to see that potential.

00:51:37.560 --> 00:51:46.420
 But it took at least one and a half year, two years, to really coordinate an agenda between both of them and create a trust.

00:51:47.020 --> 00:51:53.320
 But between farmers, it was like open up their whole vision when they visited.

00:51:53.820 --> 00:51:56.320
 They did the farmers-to-farmer exchanges.

00:51:56.760 --> 00:52:11.300
 They went to Honduras, they went to different parts of Nicaragua, and also the farmers from PECAC visit the farmers because they wanted to know about coffee because they are cultivating more like beans and corn and vegetables and how they did the work.

00:52:11.460 --> 00:52:13.200
 And they were more open.

00:52:13.340 --> 00:52:19.880
 And after the second exchange, everything started, you know, like moving.

00:52:20.000 --> 00:52:29.560
 I call it like a spider movement, when it has too many legs and gets stuck and doesn't want to move.

00:52:29.620 --> 00:52:32.760
 But when it moves, it moves and checks the whole web.

00:52:33.620 --> 00:52:36.280
 It was like moving a network.

00:52:36.780 --> 00:52:38.840
 There was no way to control it.

00:52:39.340 --> 00:52:42.980
 And I love it because it was, okay, it doesn't matter.

00:52:42.980 --> 00:52:50.100
 You have to keep, you know, like keep like an act of balancing, a little bit saying, okay, toward this direction or toward this other direction.

00:52:50.260 --> 00:52:52.160
 But it's no way to control it.

00:52:52.380 --> 00:52:55.160
 And as a researcher, you can't document all this.

00:52:55.240 --> 00:52:59.440
 You can only really do these periodic interviews, focus groups, and surveys and talk about part of it.

00:52:59.440 --> 00:53:11.200
 But there's, now that this sort of spirit of experimentation that was already there has started, been unleashed, we, you know, and who knows what's going to, I mean, we want to systematize a little bit of it.

00:53:11.200 --> 00:53:21.200
 But that sort of, that sort of alive experimental aspect of their work, and it's also seen through the seed backs, is really right.

00:53:21.260 --> 00:53:22.260
 Yeah, and the training.

00:53:22.560 --> 00:53:25.140
 I think the training, they are different kind of trainings.

00:53:25.520 --> 00:53:34.580
 Not just the farmers-to-farmer exchanges, but I mentioned that the farmers-led experimentation is part of the training too.

00:53:35.320 --> 00:53:41.060
 And there are at least 700 farmers involved in the experimentation.

00:53:41.560 --> 00:53:44.660
 They were only eight in the beginning.

00:53:45.720 --> 00:53:50.250
 Because it's, we didn't know, it's a good methodology.

00:53:50.650 --> 00:53:52.170
 Are they open to do that?

00:53:52.410 --> 00:53:53.950
 How we will reframe this?

00:53:54.230 --> 00:53:57.180
 What will be with the academic criterias?

00:53:57.400 --> 00:54:04.260
 Or will be with the more like open criterias from, because they are science too, but from farmers' perspective?

00:54:04.580 --> 00:54:06.360
 And how we will do the combination?

00:54:06.660 --> 00:54:08.400
 And we have been applying the combination.

00:54:08.400 --> 00:54:19.010
 Say, okay, this we will call open experimentation, and the other we call more academic experimentation, that are more like led by, they have more restrictions.

00:54:19.470 --> 00:54:25.510
 So like replicated plot trials for an agroecological sort of milkman management.

00:54:25.610 --> 00:54:30.130
 Because a lot of farmers, when we start out, wanted to see, well, how effective are all these organic strategies?

00:54:30.130 --> 00:54:43.750
 I mean, people are used to applying a little bit of herbicide and some oreo, even though they're growing organic coffee, they're used to applying some herbicide and some oreo, which is nitrogen fertilizer to the corn, if they have it on hand, right?

00:54:43.810 --> 00:54:50.450
 And so we set up a side-by-side experiment to compare these two that was really organized.

00:54:50.570 --> 00:54:55.200
 This was really set up more through the project than through the university, but it was to compare these results.

00:54:55.200 --> 00:54:57.020
 So it's kind of more of a systematic approach.

00:54:57.020 --> 00:55:04.040
 But the much bigger part has been the open experimentation, really, that draws heavily on the Campasino-Campasino approach.

00:55:04.360 --> 00:55:22.410
 And I just want to say before we continue that the polling – so the idea of uniting that some of the social process methodology of Campasino-Campasino with the organizational capacity and the export of the fair trade co-ops is something that's been, in my mind, and probably others for a long time, many, right?

00:55:22.780 --> 00:55:27.510
 But as Mario Henia said, it hasn't come together in practice because that's the hard work, right?

00:55:27.870 --> 00:55:38.450
 I want to give a shout-out to Eric Holt-Gimenez from Food First because when – in the early stages of this work, I knew we needed somebody on the ground to help make part of this happen.

00:55:38.590 --> 00:55:45.430
 And he suggested a member of the National Coordinating Committee, Jorge Heran, from Campasino-Campasino.

00:55:45.550 --> 00:55:46.110
 In Nicaragua.

00:55:46.190 --> 00:55:47.790
 In Nicaragua, who's from there.

00:55:48.010 --> 00:56:00.680
 And he was willing to work as an advisor to this initiative and really has been a key player together with the real openness on some of the co-op rural development and agronomy team.

00:56:01.130 --> 00:56:02.990
 But I do remember some of those early meetings, right?

00:56:03.030 --> 00:56:07.340
 And he's like, look, if we're talking about movement, we're cooperative movements, no problem.

00:56:07.550 --> 00:56:10.660
 But like Campasino movement, I'm not so sure, right?

00:56:10.760 --> 00:56:14.060
 And so there's not like there is just this big embrace.

00:56:15.370 --> 00:56:28.730
 But then – and here is the technology to me and the change that really made it run, which Mario Henia has referred to a couple times already, both in terms of the donors and also in terms of the combination of these strategies.

00:56:28.730 --> 00:56:38.570
 It's really around these community-based seed banks because I still remember our first meeting up in Cantagayo above Condega with one of the donors was actually there and the agronomists were still there.

00:56:38.650 --> 00:56:40.390
 And they were still pretty much like, we are the agronomists.

00:56:40.530 --> 00:56:41.610
 We know what's going on.

00:56:42.010 --> 00:56:44.330
 You know, and they pulled – and they did some participation.

00:56:44.910 --> 00:56:48.390
 But they were – it was – you know, and they were promoting organic coffee.

00:56:48.550 --> 00:56:52.930
 But it was still pretty straight up, you know, here are the suggestions.

00:56:53.130 --> 00:56:53.550
 Follow them.

00:56:54.260 --> 00:56:55.450
 And they were talking.

00:56:55.670 --> 00:56:58.370
 I said, well, what kind of seed – what kind of varieties of corn do you have here?

00:56:58.370 --> 00:57:04.270
 And then Douglas, one of the farmers, said, well, this is Juan Vente Tres and this is Olotillo.

00:57:04.610 --> 00:57:10.280
 And this is – and he started to go through these different varieties and which one he would try to plant and when.

00:57:10.900 --> 00:57:16.680
 And at that moment, it was interesting because all of a sudden, you know, one of the agronomists was totally blind.

00:57:16.760 --> 00:57:20.820
 I mean, he did not – he couldn't get to the level of crop varieties that were adapted for the zone.

00:57:21.520 --> 00:57:28.530
 And the donor representative that has since moved on, Rick, Rick was like – he saw that knowledge that was embedded there.

00:57:28.590 --> 00:57:35.050
 And then I think once the farmers visited a community-based seed bank – this wasn't really even thought of in the initial project.

00:57:35.450 --> 00:57:39.760
 They visited one in San Ramón, which is another place where the Community Area Ecology Network works.

00:57:39.960 --> 00:57:42.900
 But it's actually – they visited a seed bank that was organized by Campasino Campasino.

00:57:43.000 --> 00:57:43.740
 They came back.

00:57:44.380 --> 00:57:48.620
 And since then, Mario Henia knows better how fast it has moved through the co-ops.

00:57:49.100 --> 00:57:55.180
 Yeah, we have seven seed banks established in the Las Segovia Project.

00:57:55.620 --> 00:58:00.880
 There are other projects in Campasino that also had seed banks established after that.

00:58:01.650 --> 00:58:07.530
 And with ProDeCo, more than 500 farmers have been involved.

00:58:08.070 --> 00:58:11.070
 They have been receiving seeds from the seed banks.

00:58:11.070 --> 00:58:18.810
 And we are working with more than 60 varieties now between corn and beans.

00:58:19.520 --> 00:58:35.370
 And having like a powerful experience because they knew about basic grains, but they have been receiving a lot of technical support for the coffee, not for the basic grains.

00:58:35.370 --> 00:58:58.490
 And knowing the importance of having especially corn and beans during the period of hunger, they were eager to learn how to access to food, how to store food, and how to produce more food and having more seeds available that were resistant to the climate change.

00:58:58.630 --> 00:59:03.420
 That it was another challenge that we have been confronting in the last five years.

00:59:03.420 --> 00:59:15.160
 And now with the seed banks, they developed their own rules and regulations to minimize it.

00:59:15.220 --> 00:59:27.730
 They have the benefit, but it's also already the structure established that was adding a new strategy in how to control their own food system.

00:59:27.970 --> 00:59:34.100
 And there are other cops that are interested in establishing the seed banks.

00:59:34.300 --> 00:59:40.810
 But sometimes it takes a while, you know, to go to negotiate with all the members because it's a democracy.

00:59:41.660 --> 00:59:43.820
 You have to go through the whole process.

00:59:45.400 --> 00:59:52.660
 But after everything got established, it is a powerful strategy, I would say.

00:59:53.660 --> 01:00:04.180
 And we have been facing with the government in Nicaragua and all the business who sell seeds, they prepare the whole package.

01:00:04.440 --> 01:00:09.280
 They say the improved seed plus the fertilizer plus all the kind of things.

01:00:09.840 --> 01:00:16.340
 And the agronomists say, what if we have to prepare like our ecological package too?

01:00:16.800 --> 01:00:20.560
 Because they are having a big competition on the ground.

01:00:20.760 --> 01:00:29.140
 And when you see a farmer that is not completely convinced, it's just easy to go and get into that and buy the whole package.

01:00:29.560 --> 01:00:37.020
 But when you have a farmer that is 100% convinced, there's no way that he will buy those kind of packages.

01:00:37.500 --> 01:00:49.360
 And now we have the whole group of farmers that are like preaching to each other, you know, saying, okay, we will show you with this proof how this seed.

01:00:49.360 --> 01:01:02.900
 I met the one from San Antonio that he is so happy that during the last drought, 2014, he said, no, the other farmers, they planted their seeds.

01:01:03.040 --> 01:01:12.600
 They didn't have a harvest, but I was able to have 12 quintals or this type of corn from the parcel that I planted.

01:01:12.980 --> 01:01:16.460
 And people look at me and say, are you like performing miracles?

01:01:17.040 --> 01:01:22.100
 And I say, yes, it's the type of seed that I got.

01:01:22.220 --> 01:01:27.140
 And I gave some of the seed to other farmers and he was able to have food.

01:01:27.220 --> 01:01:39.720
 And he was so happy sharing the result of his experimentation, saying, here is the corn, the varieties, and my son, they call it, because it's a big, big seed.

01:01:39.720 --> 01:01:43.750
 And he said, here is, we are able to have food.

01:01:44.050 --> 01:01:49.830
 And you see how farmers are, is empowering them.

01:01:52.050 --> 01:02:00.930
 And when they say, okay, we are able to fulfill this goal, not for the project, it's for myself and for my family.

01:02:01.550 --> 01:02:04.850
 And I want to share this knowledge with other farmers.

01:02:04.850 --> 01:02:15.530
 And this is, for me, revolutionary, you know, being open, because there is a moment we were so tired of being volunteers during the revolution.

01:02:15.710 --> 01:02:22.940
 There was a moment in Nicaragua history that nobody wanted to keep volunteering for different activities.

01:02:23.450 --> 01:02:39.230
 And now seeing this attitude about, I want to share, because I know that the other farmers are living through this situation, and I want to share that you have the power to produce food for your family and for your community.

01:02:39.750 --> 01:02:43.970
 And I'm just fascinated with the whole thing.

01:02:44.050 --> 01:02:48.050
 Of course, there are challenges about power, relationships, and other things.

01:02:48.670 --> 01:02:57.950
 But I think acknowledging them, you know, saying, embracing the existence, just denying them doesn't work.

01:02:58.510 --> 01:03:02.170
 And they are, you know, multiples and multiple levels and multiple directions.

01:03:03.790 --> 01:03:07.300
 And your role is just facilitating.

01:03:08.250 --> 01:03:20.060
 I was talking with the farmers one time about the, when you, if you attend a concert, you see the, you hear the concert, you hear one music, beautiful music.

01:03:20.720 --> 01:03:24.480
 And you don't know how much work is behind that concert.

01:03:25.250 --> 01:03:29.410
 You know, how much the director has been working to orchestrate.

01:03:29.410 --> 01:03:47.950
 The director doesn't have to play every single instrument, but he needs to know how to support each musician to, in order for this musician to share the best of him with his instrument or her instrument.

01:03:48.890 --> 01:03:56.340
 And when you see every musician playing the best they can do for the concert, it's just amazing.

01:03:57.000 --> 01:04:01.010
 And now they are like, they call participatory concerts.

01:04:01.010 --> 01:04:07.230
 When even the public can be engaged in the whole concert, it's another level.

01:04:08.240 --> 01:04:15.560
 And, and because I have a background as a musician, all the time I try to see, you know, like the, the performance of music everywhere.

01:04:16.150 --> 01:04:24.320
 And, and I think the power for the, for the communities is easier to get it to say, okay, I don't have to become a violinist.

01:04:24.460 --> 01:04:30.490
 I don't have to become a, I don't have to conduct some surveys.

01:04:30.770 --> 01:04:37.360
 I don't have to go and do this and I don't have to go and do this and that, but I am still a farmer and I can put the best of me as a farmer.

01:04:37.880 --> 01:04:50.000
 And they start opening up and leading their own research as a farmer and finding their own, finding as a farmer and sharing with everyone and trusting each other.

01:04:50.380 --> 01:04:56.860
 No, they are not waiting for Chris saying, okay, Chris will come back during his summer break and share his results with us.

01:04:57.080 --> 01:05:03.460
 But they also celebrate when we show up and say, here is the summary, our finding, the statistical analysis.

01:05:03.960 --> 01:05:22.330
 And they say, okay, you know what, last summer that we were presenting results, one of the farmers say, I am grateful for those type of moment, because some people give you money, but when people give you ideas, ideas are more valuable than money.

01:05:22.550 --> 01:05:27.110
 But you give me a little bit of money with the good idea is also even better.

01:05:30.250 --> 01:05:32.310
 Yeah, I'd say that's very true.

01:05:32.310 --> 01:05:55.290
 And I think that the openness, I mean, we really, the bigger change, as is often the case, I think is from the side of the researcher or the development worker to not only to want to do the participation enough to like adjust the agenda, to do something that's inconvenient, you know, like are not going to give you credit because you believe in what's going to happen, right?

01:05:56.290 --> 01:05:59.050
 Translating the posters and giving them back and explaining.

01:05:59.710 --> 01:06:09.550
 And then you realize the payment is not, you know, that your publication was downloaded one more time or reached a stage, which is all important to getting to a wider eye.

01:06:09.610 --> 01:06:16.630
 And just when the farmers will say something like that or say, look, I really want you one time where last time we were there, we're having a pretty serious conversation.

01:06:16.630 --> 01:06:30.410
 Because right now in Central America, you've got a combination of a drought going into its second year with massive crop failures and the whole dry quarter from, you know, southwestern, western Nicaragua, northwestern Nicaragua, El Salvador, parts of Guatemala.

01:06:30.650 --> 01:06:36.390
 You've got going into the second year of a drought, you know, crop harvests are a little bit up this year from this first season.

01:06:36.390 --> 01:06:50.050
 But in general, 20, 30, even 40, 50 percent, you know, crop failure for corn and bean harvest combined with the coffee leaf rust, which just ravaged through the area, you know, over 50 percent of the farms in the region have been affected over the last three years.

01:06:50.050 --> 01:06:58.170
 And people have lost in the survey we did, they lost, you know, last year that people had lost from 50 more than half the farmers had lost more than half of their coffee.

01:06:58.940 --> 01:07:01.560
 So that's your cash income and then your subsistence crops.

01:07:01.720 --> 01:07:07.920
 So this is, again, diversification, those fruit trees and the work of the women and everything is even more important in these circumstances, right?

01:07:08.870 --> 01:07:14.950
 And yet they're still very open to, we're in this focus group presenting some of these results and talking about it.

01:07:15.210 --> 01:07:21.430
 And, you know, there was for the first time, I was a little worried because there's some pretty experienced farmers in it, 60 and 70 year olds.

01:07:21.530 --> 01:07:28.810
 And they're like, there was a sense that there wasn't, they were like, Chris, this is worse than we've seen it in a long time.

01:07:28.810 --> 01:07:33.230
 You know, we don't like to ask for, you know, food assistance, right?

01:07:33.350 --> 01:07:37.930
 And now they can ask for it to some extent through their co-op.

01:07:38.150 --> 01:07:42.810
 And we'd finished the whole meeting, we were about to go, and a couple of the farmers came up to me.

01:07:42.870 --> 01:07:47.370
 And they had, you know, they'd rebuilt their whole cooperative building for their first time and they built one.

01:07:48.290 --> 01:07:50.770
 And they're like, Chris, we want to ask you for something.

01:07:51.630 --> 01:07:54.030
 And I'm like, oh, you know, like what's this going to be?

01:07:54.030 --> 01:08:00.270
 Because, you know, we get asked as being, you know, with lighter skin in Central America, usually people ask for money or whatever.

01:08:00.470 --> 01:08:01.530
 And I had no, or, you know.

01:08:01.690 --> 01:08:03.110
 Well, they don't know you.

01:08:03.270 --> 01:08:06.870
 But they knew me, so I figured it probably wouldn't be that direct, right?

01:08:06.870 --> 01:08:08.170
 I didn't know what they were going to ask for.

01:08:08.270 --> 01:08:12.610
 Like, you know, can you help leverage another expansion of the project, which is really out of my hands.

01:08:13.140 --> 01:08:15.070
 They said, you know what, is there anybody you can talk to?

01:08:15.130 --> 01:08:17.950
 Because we really want one of these seed banks that everybody's been talking about.

01:08:18.010 --> 01:08:19.970
 We want to have access to these seeds.

01:08:20.470 --> 01:08:23.110
 And to me, that was really powerful because they weren't asking.

01:08:23.690 --> 01:08:28.410
 They were asking for, they had the knowledge and the access to these resources.

01:08:28.410 --> 01:08:42.500
 And I think the next step in this project is really to work with a down-to-earth, highly trained participatory seed specialist who really can get into the nitty-gritty of the varieties, what types of work, what kinds of crosses from different regions can work.

01:08:42.500 --> 01:08:50.070
 And pull genetic material in, but also respect the ownership, the history, and the aspirations of the farmers in this process.

01:08:50.170 --> 01:08:52.930
 So it's not a closed kind of internal-only strategy.

01:08:53.410 --> 01:09:03.400
 But it's a delicate process where the introduction of new participants needs to be vetted by all those that are actively involved.

01:09:04.700 --> 01:09:10.920
 And I want to add about the training and surveys and youth.

01:09:12.360 --> 01:09:18.500
 And I think without planning it, we have been training a team.

01:09:19.170 --> 01:09:20.330
 Yeah, that's right.

01:09:20.510 --> 01:09:21.070
 A whole team.

01:09:21.390 --> 01:09:28.650
 And they are young people that have the energy and the enthusiasm.

01:09:29.450 --> 01:09:33.530
 And I think some of them were in high school when we started.

01:09:33.870 --> 01:09:45.040
 And we tried to, and this was part of the, not as a part of the project, but part of the commitment, to look for funding for scholarships.

01:09:46.130 --> 01:09:49.340
 And 10 of them have been receiving scholarships.

01:09:50.190 --> 01:09:55.070
 And I think at least 15 food security promoters.

01:09:55.190 --> 01:10:04.550
 And in the beginning, they were like enthusiastic youth who want to participate in some ways inside the whole initiative.

01:10:05.110 --> 01:10:09.590
 And we started with the diagnostics saying, okay, we will design a survey, apply the survey.

01:10:09.850 --> 01:10:15.830
 But after that, because we went back, we have the whole monitoring system.

01:10:16.820 --> 01:10:23.940
 Besides all the research that we have been conducting together with Chris, every year we apply different surveys.

01:10:24.140 --> 01:10:30.070
 We have internal data and the outside data that we have been doing.

01:10:30.190 --> 01:10:35.870
 And we have been inviting other people who have been conducting research in the same area, too, with the same project.

01:10:36.170 --> 01:10:40.510
 But they have been receiving at least five years of training.

01:10:41.170 --> 01:10:51.230
 And you can see how they can, if you show up saying, okay, I want to apply this survey, they will just put your survey upside down.

01:10:51.350 --> 01:10:53.450
 Say, this question doesn't apply.

01:10:53.710 --> 01:11:01.050
 Because we have been discussing with them how to adapt a specific tool and what is the language.

01:11:01.050 --> 01:11:03.130
 The appropriate language from the existing survey.

01:11:03.350 --> 01:11:03.910
 Exactly.

01:11:04.230 --> 01:11:11.950
 With the coping mechanisms, we went through like at least three times the application during two years saying, okay, this question is not appropriated.

01:11:12.330 --> 01:11:21.950
 This question has to be analyzed in a different way, has to be asked in a different way, or the translation was not appropriated.

01:11:22.370 --> 01:11:24.610
 Or we have been readjusting all.

01:11:24.610 --> 01:11:30.410
 They have been learning about methods, about methodology, about analysis.

01:11:31.350 --> 01:11:41.190
 Getting the letters, the idea of being trained about what is the ethical conduct of research, of getting oral and then written permission from the people that they're surveying.

01:11:41.950 --> 01:11:48.640
 And also, they know that we will hear them when they have opinions.

01:11:49.340 --> 01:12:03.730
 In the beginning, they were really shy, saying, oh, maybe you have to ask this and not ask if they want to send their kids to the neighbor's family to be fed.

01:12:04.070 --> 01:12:06.970
 Because it doesn't sound right.

01:12:06.970 --> 01:12:10.510
 And now say, can we take out this question from this survey?

01:12:10.730 --> 01:12:11.670
 It's not appropriated.

01:12:13.430 --> 01:12:19.610
 And you can see when we get together, they are sometimes equal prepared as us.

01:12:19.810 --> 01:12:21.610
 They go with their copy of the survey.

01:12:21.870 --> 01:12:26.790
 They tie all their notes, everything they have been doing during the whole last six months.

01:12:26.950 --> 01:12:34.710
 And we sat down, they already know what time we arrive, that is during the summer break here, that is between June and July for us, or around December.

01:12:34.970 --> 01:12:35.890
 They already know.

01:12:36.350 --> 01:12:37.470
 And they are prepared.

01:12:37.820 --> 01:12:38.780
 They are waiting.

01:12:38.920 --> 01:12:40.680
 And they will accommodate their agenda.

01:12:40.840 --> 01:12:46.480
 We have to negotiate the agenda before we arrive, saying, OK, because they have to negotiate with their universities.

01:12:47.040 --> 01:12:58.020
 Now they are called food security promoters, and they have a title, and part of the extensions of the agronomists.

01:12:58.160 --> 01:13:00.300
 And they're all paid through the cooperative structure.

01:13:00.660 --> 01:13:02.000
 And they're getting scholarships.

01:13:02.580 --> 01:13:03.580
 Yeah, it's more like a stipend.

01:13:03.720 --> 01:13:06.400
 But they're getting scholarships to pay for part of their education.

01:13:06.600 --> 01:13:07.080
 So a lot of these.

01:13:07.180 --> 01:13:08.520
 So now you have, this is the interesting thing.

01:13:08.560 --> 01:13:17.200
 You have children of coffee farmers, some of which are illiterate, many of which have average, I think the average in the last survey is maybe three or four years of primary school education.

01:13:17.760 --> 01:13:21.940
 So their children are now graduating from four-year and five-year degrees in universities.

01:13:21.940 --> 01:13:27.680
 So I think what's interesting then is that they are now graduating from the university.

01:13:28.100 --> 01:13:35.960
 And they're thinking they're going to bring in the ideas that they've learned from their youth, from their childhood, from their parents, and from their grandparents.

01:13:36.730 --> 01:13:45.450
 And what they've learned through working with the cooperatives and the specific initiative and some of the training for conducting research.

01:13:45.590 --> 01:13:48.290
 And I'm really excited to see where they take this.

01:13:48.370 --> 01:13:51.270
 And I want to add one more piece, which is part of the intercultural piece.

01:13:51.350 --> 01:13:59.300
 Through my research at Santa Clara University, we have been, when we do these surveys, the only reason we're able to really get the kind of reach.

01:13:59.440 --> 01:14:01.540
 And I think participatory action research expands.

01:14:01.540 --> 01:14:20.580
 There's a great article by Rachel Morales of Frosch from Berkeley and Carolina Balsas that talks about how the participatory action research expands the reach, the relevance, and the rigor because of the research, because of the actual engagement in terms of questions that people care about.

01:14:21.040 --> 01:14:35.300
 And I think we've explained that already through the way survey questions are cross-checked and the reach because we can reach, if you're working with 10 or 12 youth promoters who are farmers themselves, you can reach 350 farmers on a very low budget with a little bit of seed money, right?

01:14:35.380 --> 01:14:37.780
 And some of the monitoring support from the CAN project.

01:14:38.880 --> 01:14:43.660
 And the relevance is because you're asking questions about their own lives.

01:14:43.720 --> 01:14:56.560
 But I think what's also cool is what we do every summer is connect this also to what I'm teaching, the food justice class at Santa Clara University, but also we bring two students down from Santa Clara, and in the future we'll bring more.

01:14:57.640 --> 01:15:00.900
 And these students come, and before they go, they do lots of work.

01:15:00.980 --> 01:15:13.340
 And it's probably more of the most relevant work is what they do before they go in terms of, like, sorting through survey data, cross-checking it, data cleaning, all that kind of fun stuff, looking for follow-up questions, designing initial ideas.

01:15:13.340 --> 01:15:19.940
 That are, you know, internationally comparable on survey methods or research questions, or working on this water question.

01:15:20.020 --> 01:15:25.460
 We haven't talked a lot about water, topic for future work, but the way water intersects with gender and food security is huge.

01:15:25.580 --> 01:15:35.190
 So these students come down for usually just two or three weeks, but they come embedded in this whole set of relationships that have been formed over years, a decade, if you think about it.

01:15:35.810 --> 01:15:40.750
 And they're able to work side-by-side with the youth from Nicaragua in doing some of this research, right?

01:15:40.750 --> 01:15:48.150
 And then they come back, and they have the time to sit at the desk of the GIS system and, like, process the data and present professional posters.

01:15:48.290 --> 01:15:54.010
 And it's been really cool for me to see where these students, also on the Santa Clara side, come back and what they do.

01:15:54.090 --> 01:16:00.390
 So I'm as interested in what the children of these coffee farmers do now as these Santa Clara students that come back that are about the same age.

01:16:00.430 --> 01:16:01.550
 And I can tell you some of the stories.

01:16:01.690 --> 01:16:09.750
 I mean, the first guy that came, Ian, he came down, and he graduated, double major in environmental studies and political science.

01:16:09.790 --> 01:16:10.930
 He thought about things for a while.

01:16:11.030 --> 01:16:12.090
 He talked to a lot of friends.

01:16:12.550 --> 01:16:15.690
 He did a little follow-up research assistance work with Community Ivory Ecology Network.

01:16:16.190 --> 01:16:20.210
 And eventually, I get this call from United Farm Workers saying, you know, do you know Ian Doherty?

01:16:20.410 --> 01:16:21.470
 And a reference check.

01:16:21.550 --> 01:16:24.970
 And so he's worked the last year and a half with United Farm Workers as a community organizer.

01:16:25.550 --> 01:16:27.110
 Another student, Rika, came down.

01:16:27.670 --> 01:16:31.350
 She went and is currently working in Washington, D.C. at the Pew Trust.

01:16:31.530 --> 01:16:35.970
 So people – and she took – it was a co-author on a peer-reviewed publication, a top-tier journal.

01:16:36.430 --> 01:16:44.530
 So she took these kinds of experiences, and they're talking about these experiences as they're applying to graduate school and law school and some of the top programs across the United States.

01:16:44.990 --> 01:16:55.370
 So I'm really – and I'm wondering the depth of the transformation that happened for both the students that have gone down as well as the, you know, the students from Nicaraguan University,

01:16:55.710 --> 01:17:00.470
 children of coffee farmers, and what – where they're going to take some of these ideas going forward.

01:17:00.470 --> 01:17:01.910
 And that's exciting to me.

01:17:02.110 --> 01:17:14.830
 And we just received, you know, three years of support from the National Science Foundation to do a deeper analysis of this impact of food and water insecurity in the region and also train another group of students from the Santa Clara side.

01:17:15.010 --> 01:17:19.350
 And with – hopefully with CAN, we will match them with at least equal number of Nicaraguan students.

01:17:19.870 --> 01:17:21.670
 So I'm excited about that.

01:17:22.230 --> 01:17:23.710
 And it's beyond language.

01:17:23.710 --> 01:17:28.870
 Just for the audience here who are university students.

01:17:29.630 --> 01:17:42.740
 Students, when we have the interviews with them, it's like they are concerned about language and culture and other things.

01:17:42.900 --> 01:17:46.140
 Most of them, they didn't speak Spanish.

01:17:46.780 --> 01:17:47.810
 And they knew that.

01:17:47.810 --> 01:17:54.940
 And they were able, of course, to ask for a little bit of food that they were hungry, a little bit of water.

01:17:54.940 --> 01:17:58.980
 But they were not really able to communicate in Spanish.

01:17:59.680 --> 01:18:10.440
 But after their visit, they realized that the language was not the main barrier, that they were able to communicate in some ways.

01:18:11.060 --> 01:18:20.480
 And especially with Ian, I think still the farmers still talk about him because he say, see, see, for every question.

01:18:20.620 --> 01:18:21.540
 I mean, yes, yes.

01:18:22.000 --> 01:18:23.020
 And they say, make fun.

01:18:23.100 --> 01:18:26.390
 But they noticed that he has a good heart.

01:18:27.570 --> 01:18:30.690
 And they didn't look at what he was just nodding, no?

01:18:30.950 --> 01:18:33.270
 But what he was willing to do.

01:18:33.980 --> 01:18:36.780
 Say, can you, like, explain me how to use this cell phone?

01:18:36.780 --> 01:18:45.260
 Because we were, in that moment, we were doing, like, a pilot project using cell phones to gather the data from the TALAS.

01:18:45.660 --> 01:18:47.000
 They're community-based brain banks.

01:18:47.420 --> 01:18:47.700
 Yes.

01:18:48.140 --> 01:18:50.830
 And they say, yes, yes.

01:18:51.280 --> 01:18:52.750
 But he didn't know how to do it.

01:18:54.090 --> 01:18:54.950
 Then he figured it out.

01:18:55.130 --> 01:18:55.990
 They figured it out.

01:18:56.090 --> 01:18:57.350
 But it was a challenge for him.

01:18:57.690 --> 01:18:59.390
 And then he was really proud.

01:18:59.950 --> 01:19:02.930
 When he, I will say, the visit was important.

01:19:03.490 --> 01:19:04.550
 And he learned a lot.

01:19:04.610 --> 01:19:06.250
 And the farmers, too, learned a lot.

01:19:06.770 --> 01:19:07.070
 But...

01:19:07.230 --> 01:19:15.670
 Was in the way back, when he put a lot of time to go through all the data and help us analyze the data.

01:19:15.670 --> 01:19:19.730
 And when we went back and presented the data and saying, okay, this is Ian's work.

01:19:20.240 --> 01:19:21.810
 He has a couple of graphics.

01:19:22.270 --> 01:19:23.650
 They celebrate that.

01:19:23.870 --> 01:19:25.570
 Say, oh, the student, he's working.

01:19:26.470 --> 01:19:36.610
 And we now are having some clear data that we are able to understand thanks to three months or four months of work that he put, like, going through the whole data.

01:19:37.150 --> 01:19:40.140
 And I think it is a possibility.

01:19:40.140 --> 01:19:42.660
 We will end it with the same words, no?

01:19:42.740 --> 01:19:45.900
 It is a possibility, always, of collaboration.

01:19:46.580 --> 01:19:49.340
 Always a possibility for solidarity.

01:19:50.620 --> 01:19:52.760
 It was always a possibility for peace.

01:19:53.180 --> 01:19:58.770
 Always for, I will say, cohesion and interaction.

01:19:59.800 --> 01:20:01.700
 You know, it's not equal opportunities.

01:20:01.700 --> 01:20:05.450
 But we have to look for it.

01:20:05.530 --> 01:20:08.280
 Sometimes they are not, like, visible there.

01:20:08.580 --> 01:20:10.390
 It's not the first thing that you notice.

01:20:10.960 --> 01:20:18.270
 But we have to put, we have to look for it and search for it in order to find it.

01:20:18.590 --> 01:20:20.840
 Thanks so much.

01:20:20.960 --> 01:20:21.740
 That was great.

01:20:21.740 --> 01:20:27.690
 As always, we mentioned a lot of different organizations and papers and everything.

01:20:27.770 --> 01:20:36.570
 We'll have links to all of those and to the Community Agroecology Network and other organizations that you work with in the show notes, that deliciousrevolutionshow.com.

01:20:36.670 --> 01:20:44.160
 And thanks so much for your time and for talking about revolution and collaboration and food security with me.

01:20:44.490 --> 01:20:45.780
 Thank you for the invitation.

01:20:46.270 --> 01:20:47.160
 Thank you so much.

01:20:47.240 --> 01:20:47.660
 Our pleasure.

